\description{
\code{GlobalAdjoint(alg)} wraps an ODE algorithm with adjoint based global
error estimation and control (Cao and Petzold 2004), as GlobalDiffEq.jl.
The problem is solved with local tolerances, the 2-norm of the endpoint
error is estimated from \code{samples} orthogonal random projections, and the
local tolerances (which default to \code{gtol}) are tightened until the
estimate is at most \code{gtol}. With more than one state the estimate is
probabilistic; two samples are accurate within a factor of ten with
probability above 99\%. With as many samples as states it is the norm of
the full error estimate.
}
\details{
For a projection direction \code{v} the error component \verb{<v, y(T) - P(T)>} is
the dual weighted residual, the integral of \verb{lambda(t)' (f(P) - P')}
with the adjoint \code{lambda(T) = v}, \code{P} the dense numerical solution and
\verb{f(P) - P'} its defect. It is computed by \code{\link[=adjoint_sensitivities]{adjoint_sensitivities()}} as
the sensitivity to a scalar forcing that adds the defect to the
dynamics; \code{sensealg} (by default \code{\link[=InterpolatingAdjoint]{InterpolatingAdjoint()}}) and
\code{adjoint_alg} select the adjoint method and its solver.

\code{solve(prob, GlobalAdjoint(alg, gtol = ...))} returns a solution with
\code{sol$global_error} (and \code{\link[=has_global_error]{has_global_error()}} is \code{TRUE}): with
\link{EndpointError} the estimate in its last element (the others are zero),
with \link{TrajectoryError} the estimate at every saved time. With
\link{ToleranceRefinement} the solution is an adaptive solve at the accepted
tolerances; with \link{StepGridRefinement} a fixed step solve on the accepted
step grid. \code{adjoint_error_estimate(prob, alg)} solves \code{prob} and returns
the estimate. The solver must have a dense output with a first
derivative (see \code{\link[=GlobalErrorEstimation]{GlobalErrorEstimation()}}); forward time ODEs with the
standard mass matrix and real vector states are supported.
